## Pull Request Best Practices

- Small, focused PRs reviewable in one sitting
- All tests pass before requesting review
- Follow existing patterns and conventions
- Clear description of what and why
- Link related issue tickets

---

## Nile Health pull requests (Nile-Core & NileWebApps)

Patterns observed from merged PRs and reviewer feedback in `Nile-Health/nile-core` and `Nile-Health/NileWebApps`.

### Typical size (feature work)

Exclude automated **Deploy to Production** PRs and large merge/sync PRs when judging size.

| Metric | Nile-Core | NileWebApps |
|--------|-----------|-------------|
| Median line churn (add + del) | ~130 | ~130 |
| Median files changed | ~3 | ~4 |
| Sweet spot | Under ~500 lines, few files | Same |
| Common large PRs | E2E suites, catalog features, multi-screen UI | Same |

Prefer several small PRs over one monolithic change. Automation often labels PRs **Small** vs **Large/Monolithic**; large cohesive features happen but need stronger testing notes and reviewer time.

### Nile-Core description (`pull_request_template.md`)

Use the repo template sections:

1. **Description** — what changed and why (not a file list).
2. **Migration Risk Assessment** — exactly one of:
   - **Green** — fully reversible (`create_table`, `rename_column`, `add_index`, etc.).
   - **Yellow** — reversible but heavy or long-running (`add_column` on large tables, type changes, indexes on big tables). Deploy when clinic traffic is low; include rollback/mitigation.
   - **Red** — destructive or irreversible (`drop_table`, `drop_column`, data backfills). Needs VP Engineering or Chief Architect approval.
3. **Testing** — concrete commands and scenarios (e.g. `rspec path/to/spec.rb`, manual steps).
4. **Monitoring** — how success will be observed (Datadog, logs, metrics, feature flags).

For behavior changes, **Before / After** examples (API payloads, SMS text, UI copy) help reviewers and are used in production PRs.

Agent-generated bodies often use `<!-- CURSOR_AGENT_PR_BODY_BEGIN -->` with **Summary**, **Behavior**, and **Changes** — that structure is acceptable when it maps to the template above.

### NileWebApps description

No checked-in PR template; follow team conventions:

- **Summary** or **Description** at the top.
- **Changes** — bullet list by app or area (`apps/customer-frontend/...`).
- **Linear ticket** — link `https://linear.app/general-medicine/issue/GM-XXXX/...` and put `GM-XXXX` in the title when applicable.
- **Backend PR** — link the matching Nile-Core PR when the feature spans repos.
- **Testing** — manual steps, Vitest/Playwright commands, staging URLs; for hot paths, note testing **without** mocked prices/auth/data (reviewers ask for this explicitly).
- Optional **CURSOR_SUMMARY** risk block (Low/Medium/High) — align the PR scope with the stated risk.

### What reviewers comment on

Address these proactively before requesting review:

| Theme | What to do |
|-------|------------|
| **Scope & simplicity** | Smallest change that solves the problem; avoid drive-by refactors. |
| **Tests** | RSpec (Core) or Vitest/Playwright (WebApps); scoped examples, not suite-wide `Model.count`. |
| **Hot paths** | Customer checkout, visit flow, booking options — describe real staging/manual verification, not only Cursor stubs. |
| **Auth & redirects** | Middleware and login return paths are high-risk; explain early-return vs `redirect` behavior. |
| **Privacy** | No patient PHI in logs, Segment, or third parties without a BAA; Datadog may be called out as in-scope when justified. |
| **DB & migrations** | Correct migration tier, indexes for new queries, two-phase deprecate-before-drop; yellow/red timing. |
| **Logging** | Structured `logger` / SemanticLogger — no interpolated exception messages with PHI. |
| **API & mocks** | Response shape should match product contracts (Figma/API); delete dead mocks and unused exports. |
| **External APIs** | Athena/Vital/Stripe timeouts, rate limits, cost per call in non-prod. |
| **Nits** | Import order, move helpers to top of file, reuse existing formatters (`formatCentsToDollars`, etc.) — fix nits without debate unless architectural. |
| **Product judgment** | UX bounds (e.g. "50+"), default selections — flag for standup when unclear. |
| **Cross-repo** | Link sibling PR; keep frontend and backend merge order clear in the description. |

Review summaries are often short: **LGTM**, **✅**, or a few targeted questions. Thread replies should resolve or explain (e.g. why a dependency stays).

Automated review (Bugbot, security/reviewability bots) is common — triage findings; fix real issues; don't silence valid medium+ security or duplicate-SMS/redirect bugs.

### PRs to avoid editing manually

**Deploy to Production** PRs are automated from `develop` → production: do not hand-merge; they auto-merge after staging tests. Do not use deploy PRs as the model for feature PR size or description style.

### General preferences

- Titles: imperative, specific (`Add pharmacy hours to retail SMS`, `[GM-5350] Visitron E2E tests`).
- One logical change per PR; backend and frontend split when reviewable separately.
- CI green before review; mention spec paths in the description.
- Credentials / `config/credentials.yml.enc` conflicts: rebase carefully; prefer isolating secrets changes in their own commit when possible.

